        "\n",
        "## Resumen Estructurado — Congreso BiSide 2026\n",
        "\n",
        "**ESTRUCTURA Y EVOLUCIÓN TEMPORAL DE REDES DE MULTIMORBILIDAD EN PATOLOGÍA ENDOCRINA HOSPITALARIA EN CHILE, 2019-2024**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Introducción:** Las enfermedades endocrinas, nutricionales y metabólicas (CIE-10 E00-E89) constituyen una causa frecuente de hospitalización con elevada multimorbilidad, cuya estructura de agrupamiento permanece insuficientemente descrita en el contexto hospitalario chileno. El análisis de redes de co-ocurrencia permite identificar patrones sistémicos de asociación entre comorbilidades que trascienden el enfoque bivariado convencional.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Materiales y Métodos:** Estudio retrospectivo de 128.108 egresos con diagnóstico principal endocrino del sistema GRD de Chile (2019-2024). Se extrajeron comorbilidades de campos diagnósticos secundarios (DIAGNOSTICO2-35), clasificándolas en 48 grupos clínicos agrupados en 10 categorías sistémicas (cardiovascular, metabólica, respiratoria, renal, hepática, gastrointestinal, neurológica, psiquiátrica, sustancias sanguíneas e infecciosa). Se construyeron redes de co-ocurrencia anuales con pesos de similitud de Jaccard, filtrando aristas sobre el percentil 70. Se aplicó detección de comunidades (Louvain), análisis de centralidad multidimensional (grado, intermediación, cercanía, vector propio) y descomposición k-core. Se evaluó la co-ocurrencia de neoplasias digestivas (C15-C26) como comorbilidad cruzada.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Resultados:** Se analizaron 25.289 pacientes en 2019 y 24.353 en 2024 (mortalidad global 2,15%). La hipertensión arterial esencial fue la comorbilidad dominante (48,7% en 2019 a 53,4% en 2024) y el promedio de comorbilidades aumentó de 1,60 a 2,04. La red de co-ocurrencia presentó 21 nodos y 91 aristas en 2019 (densidad 0,433; transitividad 0,660) evolucionando a 20 nodos y 93 aristas en 2024 (densidad 0,490; transitividad 0,732), reflejando una creciente interconexión. El algoritmo de Louvain identificó consistentemente 2 comunidades con modularidad baja (~0,01), indicando fuerte integración cardiovascular-metabólica. La descomposición k-core alcanzó k=9, confirmando un núcleo denso de comorbilidades interrelacionadas. La prevalencia de comorbilidad neoplásica digestiva en pacientes endocrinos aumentó de 0,66% a 0,76%.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusiones:** La estructura de redes de comorbilidad en patología endocrina revela agrupamientos clínicamente coherentes con centralidad dominante cardiovascular-metabólica y creciente densificación temporal. El bajo valor de modularidad refleja la naturaleza sistémica e integrada de la multimorbilidad endocrina.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Palabras clave:** análisis de redes, comorbilidad, enfermedades endocrinas, co-ocurrencia, multimorbilidad.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note appearance=\"minimal\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Structured Abstract — BiSide 2026 Congress\n",
        "\n",
        "**STRUCTURE AND TEMPORAL EVOLUTION OF MULTIMORBIDITY NETWORKS IN ENDOCRINE HOSPITAL PATHOLOGY IN CHILE, 2019-2024**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Introduction:** Endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (ICD-10 E00-E89) represent a major cause of hospitalization with high multimorbidity burden, whose clustering structure remains insufficiently described in the Chilean hospital context. Co-occurrence network analysis enables identification of systemic association patterns that transcend conventional bivariate approaches.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Materials and Methods:** Retrospective study of 128,108 discharges with endocrine primary diagnosis from the Chilean GRD system (2019-2024). Comorbidities were extracted from secondary diagnostic fields (DIAGNOSTICO2-35) and classified into 48 clinical groups across 10 systemic categories (cardiovascular, metabolic, respiratory, renal, hepatic, gastrointestinal, neurological, psychiatric, blood substances, and infectious). Annual co-occurrence networks were constructed with Jaccard similarity weights, filtering edges above the 70th percentile. Louvain community detection, multidimensional centrality analysis (degree, betweenness, closeness, eigenvector), and k-core decomposition were applied. Digestive neoplasm (C15-C26) co-occurrence was evaluated as cross-disease comorbidity.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Results:** A total of 25,289 patients in 2019 and 24,353 in 2024 were analyzed (overall mortality 2.15%). Essential hypertension was the dominant comorbidity (48.7% in 2019 to 53.4% in 2024) and mean comorbidity count increased from 1.60 to 2.04. The co-occurrence network comprised 21 nodes and 91 edges in 2019 (density 0.433, transitivity 0.660), evolving to 20 nodes and 93 edges in 2024 (density 0.490, transitivity 0.732), reflecting increasing interconnection. Louvain algorithm consistently identified 2 communities with low modularity (~0.01), indicating strong cardiovascular-metabolic integration. K-core decomposition reached k=9, confirming a dense core of interrelated comorbidities. Digestive neoplastic comorbidity prevalence in endocrine patients increased from 0.66% to 0.76%.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusions:** Comorbidity network structure in endocrine pathology reveals clinically coherent clusters with dominant cardiovascular-metabolic centrality and progressive temporal densification. The low modularity reflects the systemic and integrated nature of endocrine multimorbidity.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Keywords:** network analysis, comorbidity, endocrine diseases, co-occurrence, multimorbidity.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "# Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "Endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (ICD-10 E00–E89) represent a major burden on health systems worldwide, with type 2 diabetes [@khan2020; @saeedi2019], thyroid disorders [@taylor2018], obesity [@pigeyre2016], and metabolic syndrome among the most prevalent conditions. In Chile, the prevalence of diabetes and obesity has risen steadily [@sapunar2018], and cardiovascular risk factors are highly prevalent [@diaz2019]. Understanding the comorbidity landscape of these conditions is essential for clinical management and resource planning [@valderas2009].\n",
        "\n",
        "Network medicine provides a framework for studying disease–disease relationships through co-occurrence patterns [@barabasi2011; @goh2007]. The human disease network paradigm links diseases by shared comorbidities, revealing non-trivial phenotypic associations [@hidalgo2009]. Community detection using the Louvain method [@blondel2008] identifies clusters of tightly co-occurring conditions, while centrality metrics [@freeman1978; @vandenheuvel2013] and k-core decomposition [@seidman1983] characterize the structural importance of individual comorbidities. Edge weights based on Jaccard similarity [@jaccard1901] capture pairwise co-occurrence strength, and modularity analysis [@newman2006] quantifies community structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "This analysis investigates patterns of comorbidity in hospitalizations for endocrine diseases in Chile from 2019–2024, using data from the GRD (Diagnosis-Related Groups) system [@deis2024] coded according to ICD-10 [@oms2019]. The network approach reveals how comorbidities cluster and evolve over time, with particular attention to pandemic effects (2020–2021) and the relationship between endocrine conditions and neoplastic diseases.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Methodology\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data Source and Study Population\n",
        "\n",
        "This study uses administrative hospitalization data from the GRD (Grupos Relacionados de Diagnóstico) system maintained by the Chilean Ministry of Health [@deis2024], covering the period 2019–2024. The GRD database records up to 35 diagnostic fields per admission (DIAGNOSTICO1–DIAGNOSTICO35), coded according to ICD-10 [@oms2019]. The study population includes all hospitalizations with a primary diagnosis (DIAGNOSTICO1) corresponding to endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (ICD-10 E00–E89), encompassing thyroid disorders (E00–E07) [@taylor2018], diabetes mellitus (E10–E14) [@khan2020; @saeedi2019], obesity and metabolic conditions (E65–E68) [@pigeyre2016], and other endocrine disorders.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Comorbidity Detection\n",
        "\n",
        "Comorbidities were extracted from secondary diagnostic fields (DIAGNOSTICO2–DIAGNOSTICO35) and mapped to clinically meaningful groups based on ICD-10 chapter and subchapter structure. Each comorbidity group was defined by a set of ICD-10 code prefixes covering conditions across major organ systems: cardiovascular, metabolic, respiratory, renal, hepatic, gastrointestinal, neurological, psychiatric, musculoskeletal, infectious, hematological, and substance-related. For each hospitalization, binary indicators were created for the presence of each comorbidity group. The comorbidity burden per patient was calculated as the total number of distinct comorbidity groups present.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Network Construction\n",
        "\n",
        "Comorbidity co-occurrence networks were constructed for each year (2019–2024) as undirected weighted graphs $G = (V, E, w)$, where nodes $V$ represent comorbidity groups and edges $E$ connect comorbidities that co-occur in at least one patient [@barabasi2011; @hidalgo2009]. Edge weights $w_{ij}$ were computed using the Jaccard similarity coefficient [@jaccard1901]:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$J(A,B) = \\frac{|A \\cap B|}{|A \\cup B|}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $A$ and $B$ are the sets of patients with comorbidities $i$ and $j$, respectively. To filter noise and retain only clinically relevant associations, edges with Jaccard weights below the 70th percentile of the weight distribution were removed.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Community Detection\n",
        "\n",
        "Community structure was identified using the Louvain algorithm [@blondel2008], which optimizes modularity $Q$ [@newman2006]:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$Q = \\frac{1}{2m} \\sum_{ij} \\left[ w_{ij} - \\frac{k_i k_j}{2m} \\right] \\delta(c_i, c_j)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $m$ is the total edge weight, $k_i$ is the weighted degree of node $i$, $c_i$ is the community assignment of node $i$, and $\\delta$ is the Kronecker delta. Communities represent clusters of comorbidities that co-occur more frequently with each other than with the rest of the network.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Centrality Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "Multiple centrality metrics were computed to characterize the structural importance of individual comorbidities within the network [@freeman1978; @vandenheuvel2013]: degree centrality (number of connections), betweenness centrality (frequency of occurrence on shortest paths between other nodes), closeness centrality (inverse average distance to all other nodes), and eigenvector centrality (importance based on the importance of connected nodes).\n",
        "\n",
        "## K-Core Decomposition\n",
        "\n",
        "K-core decomposition [@seidman1983] was applied to identify the maximally connected subgraphs. A $k$-core is the maximal subgraph in which every node has degree at least $k$. The coreness of each node indicates how deeply embedded it is within the dense core of the network, providing a measure of structural cohesion that complements centrality metrics.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Software\n",
        "\n",
        "All analyses were conducted in Python 3.11 using NetworkX [@hagberg2008] for graph construction and analysis, python-louvain for community detection, pandas [@mckinney2010] for data manipulation, and Matplotlib [@hunter2007] and Seaborn for visualization. The computational document was rendered using Quarto with embedded Python code blocks to ensure full reproducibility.\n",
        "\n",
