        "\\newpage\n",
        "\n",
        "# Summary and Conclusions\n",
        "\n",
        "## Key Findings\n",
        "\n",
        "This comprehensive network analysis of comorbidities in endocrine disease hospitalizations in Chile (2019-2024) reveals several important patterns:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Comorbidity Burden**: The average number of comorbidities per endocrine hospitalization patient increased steadily over the study period, with notable peaks during the pandemic years (2020-2021).\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Cardiovascular Dominance**: Cardiovascular comorbidities (essential hypertension, coronary disease, cerebrovascular disease) consistently emerge as the most prevalent and central nodes in the comorbidity networks across all years.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Metabolic Clustering**: Metabolic comorbidities (especially Type 2 diabetes, obesity, and dyslipidemia) form tight clusters in the network, reflecting strong biological and epidemiological associations.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. **Network Evolution**: The network topology shows increasing density over time, suggesting that patients increasingly present with multiple interconnected comorbidities. The pandemic period (2020-2021) shows the most dramatic changes in network structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. **Community Structure**: Louvain community detection identifies stable clinical communities centered around metabolic and cardiovascular clusters, with clear disease-specific groupings.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. **Neoplastic Comorbidity**: Approximately 2-3% of endocrine patients present with concurrent neoplastic disease diagnoses. Patients with endocrine+neoplasm comorbidity show distinct network topologies with higher comorbidity burden.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. **Central Hub Stability**: While the absolute prevalences change, the relative ranking of central comorbidities remains relatively stable, with cardiovascular conditions consistently occupying top centrality positions.\n",
        "\n",
        "8. **Temporal Trends**: Respiratory complications and renal disease show increasing prevalence trends, potentially reflecting both aging of the patient population and improved survival from acute conditions during the pandemic period.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Clinical Implications\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Integrated Care Models**: The tight clustering of comorbidities suggests the need for integrated care protocols addressing multiple disease domains simultaneously.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Risk Stratification**: Network centrality measures could inform patient risk stratification and prioritization for intensive monitoring or intervention.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Resource Planning**: The temporal evolution of comorbidity patterns provides evidence for public health planning and resource allocation in endocrinology services.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "- GRD data represents diagnosed conditions; prevalence may underestimate disease burden in underprivileged populations.\n",
        "- ICD-10 coding accuracy varies by hospital and year.\n",
        "- The analysis focuses on hospitalized patients and may not represent the general population with endocrine diseases.\n",
